WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:08.800
So, first exercise—anybody been to Panera before?&nbsp;
I love Panera. I’ve got these little kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:14.000
they can make a mess and it's kind of&nbsp;
acceptable. We try to clean up, but—

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:21.200
So Panera, one of the main focuses of their&nbsp;
menu is that you pick two. So that's what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.440
we're going to do. You get to pick two. There’s&nbsp;
a bunch of cards in the center of your table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.760
and in a minute what I'm going&nbsp;
to ask you to do is pick two.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:35.680
You can spread out the deck so you can actually&nbsp;
look and shop for the words. Pick two words that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:44.080
if combined well, could transform your year.&nbsp;
So pick two words that—if combined—and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:49.040
combination is important. Because if you&nbsp;
just get soup, you miss out on the salad.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:52.800
And so by picking two words—sometimes&nbsp;
chocolate is good and sometimes peanut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.280
butter is good—but you give me a Reese's&nbsp;
and I'm like, "Yes, give me more of that."

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.680
All right. So pick two words&nbsp;
that you feel like, if combined,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.800
could transform the rest of your year.&nbsp;
Pick them, and then once you've got them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:14.720
share it with just one other person&nbsp;
right next to you. All right—go for it.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.200
Once you’ve got two, you don’t have to wait for&nbsp;
me. I know, I know—we're all suited up. I have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.400
say, you all look sharper than the CIA. I’ve done&nbsp;
a series of sessions with the CIA, and I’m looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.800
at you—you’re taking a group picture out there—and&nbsp;
I'm like, “Dang, you’re not looking serious.”

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.760
So just because we have jackets on right&nbsp;
now, really inviting levity—there’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.200
like... you're not going to break rules,&nbsp;
you're not going to screw up video footage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.520
anything like that. So in other words,&nbsp;
you don't need to wait for all my cues.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:53.600
So feel free—if you got two cards, just with&nbsp;
the one other person, because you're kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.600 --> 00:02:11.520
splitting your table in half—share your&nbsp;
thought process behind that combination.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:49.120
All right—what are you gonna do&nbsp;
tomorrow? Hey, very good. Exactly.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:52.160
So, depending on how I’m feeling,&nbsp;
sometimes I’ll get on an airplane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.920
and when somebody turns to me and asks&nbsp;
me, "Hey, what do you do?" First of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.400
I’m like, “You don't even know&nbsp;
what landmine you just stepped on.”

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:03.440
But depending on how I’m feeling, I’ll say,&nbsp;
“Well, half my job is starting conversations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.320
and the other half is ending them.”&nbsp;
I don’t really say that too often,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.760
but I'm glad you appreciated that.&nbsp;
So I'm saying that to you now.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:19.920
Just because we're filming lots of different&nbsp;
exercises, if we had more relaxed time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:23.920
I probably wouldn't interrupt quite&nbsp;
as quickly. But for the sake of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:27.920
I'm going to be stopping a lot of&nbsp;
conversations—perhaps before their time is due.

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:32.640
And let that just be an invitation to&nbsp;
continue it over lunch, during breaks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.720
the rest of the time that you spend with&nbsp;
each other. The idea here is really that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:43.040
the conversations we start here become little&nbsp;
anchors and seeds to start future conversations.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:47.520
What happens is, when we have dialogues&nbsp;
and connections like this with people—when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.960
you pass someone in the hallway, or&nbsp;
you see a client at a grocery store,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:57.600
or whatever else—if you have some of this personal&nbsp;
information, some knowledge of who they are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:03.600
it's much more easy, and you're much more&nbsp;
likely to stop and say, “Hey, how is the blank?”

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:09.360
Right? In your roles, you get all up in somebody's&nbsp;
life, and so you have a lot of those details.

